# memory/.gitattributes — synced by sync-rules-to-projects.ps1 (command_pattern #22)
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-09-02):
# Same mechanism as tools/.gitattributes, closed one directory earlier. Every repo here has
# core.autocrlf=true and no root .gitattributes, so on checkout git writes CRLF into the working
# tree and then reports `git status` clean — it normalises CRLF back to LF when it READS the file,
# so the corruption sits on disk with nothing to show for it.
# (SHARED_LESSONS: git-itself-writes-crlf-and-then-reports-the-file-clean.)
#
# HONEST SCOPE — this is PREVENTIVE, not a fix for something broken:
# The trigger scripts in tools/ genuinely could not run with CRLF (the Workflow permission handler
# refuses any script containing a CR). memory/ holds documents, not scripts, so nothing refuses
# them. Before adding this pin I CHECKED rather than assumed: check-triggers.mjs resolves three
# rule documents out of memory/ (command_pattern.md, nick-master-workflow.md,
# CODE_REVIEW_PROCEDURE.md) and **none of its 102 checks anchors a pattern with `$`** — which is
# the one regex shape a trailing \r breaks. So CRLF here cannot turn a check green or red today.
#
# It is pinned anyway because that clean result is a property of the CURRENT checks, not of the
# directory: the next `$`-anchored pattern anybody writes would fail silently in a fresh clone and
# pass on this machine, which is the exact failure shape that already cost us a day. Pinning the
# bytes is one line; remembering this caveat forever is not.
#
# Scoped to memory/ on purpose: it pins the synced rule documents without touching the project's
# own files or its line-ending conventions.
* text eol=lf
